"""Original close-reading questions. Context summaries are not invented quotations."""
from .common import item

# Each row contains a precise question followed by marking points.
TEXTS = {
'Julius Caesar Act III': ('Drama','Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.', '''Why does Caesar reject Metellus Cimber’s petition, and what does his response reveal about his self-image?|Metellus seeks the recall of his banished brother. Caesar refuses to be moved by pleading and presents his judgement as fixed. This confidence in constancy makes his vulnerability to the conspirators ironic.
Explain why Brutus refuses to allow the conspirators to kill Antony as well. What does this reveal about his political judgement?|Brutus wants an act against tyranny rather than indiscriminate slaughter; he underestimates Antony’s independent power and assumes that Caesar’s death removes the danger. His ethical intentions accompany a serious strategic error.
State three conditions Brutus imposes on Antony’s funeral speech.|Antony must not blame the conspirators; he must acknowledge their permission; he must speak after Brutus at the same public place. Credit equivalent precise accounts of the conditions.
Why does Brutus use the contrast between his love for Caesar and his love for Rome?|He presents assassination as public duty overcoming personal affection. The contrast asks citizens to judge him as a patriot. His appeal depends on their accepting that Caesar threatened their freedom.
Explain how Antony’s repeated praise of the conspirators changes its effect during the funeral speech.|The apparently respectful description becomes ironic as Antony supplies evidence against the ambition charge. Repetition lets listeners reach the accusation themselves while Antony appears to obey the restriction on blaming the conspirators.
How does Antony use Caesar’s refusal of the crown to challenge Brutus’s argument?|Brutus claims dangerous ambition. Antony recalls Caesar refusing the crown three times, supplying a public action apparently inconsistent with that charge. It helps move the crowd from assent to doubt.
Why does Antony delay reading Caesar’s will?|Delay arouses curiosity and makes the crowd demand it. The prospect of benefits establishes a personal connection with Caesar. Antony directs the crowd while presenting himself as reluctant to inflame it.
Compare Brutus’s and Antony’s methods of addressing the citizens.|Brutus offers a concise justification based on honour, reason and civic freedom. Antony uses irony, concrete memories, wounds, the will and controlled emotion. Both seek public judgement, but Antony adapts his speech to the crowd’s developing responses.'''),
'Julius Caesar Act IV': ('Drama','The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.', '''What does the proscription scene reveal about Antony and Octavius?|They bargain over people marked for death, including relatives, and discuss using Caesar’s will to meet expenses. Political calculation contrasts with the public image of loyalty surrounding Caesar’s funeral.
Why does Antony compare Lepidus to an animal used to carry burdens?|He regards Lepidus as useful for tasks rather than as an equal political judgement-maker. The comparison shows contempt and Antony’s wish to control the partnership.
Why is Brutus angry about Lucius Pella’s case?|Brutus condemns Pella for taking bribes despite Cassius’s recommendation. He sees corrupt conduct as a betrayal of the honourable cause offered for killing Caesar. Cassius considers the practical pressures of their situation.
Explain the disagreement over money sent to Brutus’s army.|Brutus says Cassius denied funds when Brutus would not raise them dishonourably. Cassius denies deliberately refusing him and blames the messenger. Distrust turns a logistical dispute into a personal quarrel.
How is the quarrel between Brutus and Cassius resolved?|Cassius offers Brutus his dagger and exposes his hurt; Brutus softens, acknowledges anger and accepts reconciliation. Their personal affection temporarily overcomes accusations and political differences.
What does Brutus disclose about Portia, and why is the disclosure significant here?|Portia has died by swallowing fire, distressed by Brutus’s absence and the political situation. Brutus’s controlled disclosure explains part of his emotional strain and shows the personal cost of the conflict.
Give Cassius’s reason for waiting for the enemy and Brutus’s reason for marching to Philippi.|Cassius wants the enemy to tire and consume resources while their army rests. Brutus fears the enemy will gather support from the people between them and argues that they should act at a favourable moment.
How does Caesar’s ghost affect the close of Act IV?|The apparition identifies itself as Brutus’s evil spirit and promises another meeting at Philippi. Brutus questions it but cannot control it. It connects Caesar’s continuing influence with the approaching battle and Brutus’s fate.'''),
'Julius Caesar Act V': ('Drama','The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.', '''Why is Cassius disturbed by the birds accompanying the army?|He contrasts the earlier eagles with ravens, crows and kites and interprets the change as an ominous sign. His admission that he now partly credits omens shows anxiety before battle.
What disagreement occurs between Antony and Octavius about their positions in battle?|Antony directs Octavius to the left; Octavius chooses the right. Octavius’s refusal to follow the instruction reveals independence within their alliance rather than complete submission to Antony.
Why does Cassius send Titinius towards the approaching troops?|Cassius needs to know whether the troops are friends or enemies. He sends Titinius to investigate and relies on Pindarus’s distant observations instead of obtaining a reliable direct report.
Explain the error that leads Cassius to order Pindarus to kill him.|Pindarus mistakes the soldiers surrounding Titinius and their shouts for capture. Cassius concludes that his friend has been taken and his position is lost. In fact the troops are friendly and celebrate Titinius.
Why does Titinius place a garland on Cassius and then kill himself?|The garland signifies the success and honour Cassius failed to recognise. Finding Cassius dead because of a mistake, Titinius expresses loyalty and grief and uses Cassius’s sword to take his own life.
Why does Lucilius pretend to be Brutus, and how does Antony respond?|Lucilius risks capture to protect Brutus. Antony recognises the loyalty behind the deception and orders him treated well, judging him a valuable honourable prisoner rather than punishing him immediately.
How does Strato assist Brutus at the end?|After other companions refuse to kill him, Brutus asks Strato to hold the sword and runs upon it. This preserves Brutus’s sense of choosing his own end after military defeat.
Explain Antony’s final judgement of Brutus and its qualification of the play’s political conflict.|Antony distinguishes Brutus’s concern for the public good from the envy motivating other conspirators. He praises Brutus’s balanced nature and honour, acknowledging moral worth in a defeated opponent while not undoing the disastrous consequences of his act.'''),
'With the Photographer': ('Prose','A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.', '''Why is the narrator’s purpose in visiting the studio important to the conflict?|He wants a recognisable likeness of himself. The photographer assumes that technically improving the face is more important. Their incompatible definitions of a satisfactory photograph create the conflict.
How do the photographer’s comments on the narrator’s face establish his attitude?|He inspects individual features as faults and treats the customer as raw material. His professional certainty diminishes the narrator’s authority over his own appearance.
Why does the narrator insist on ownership of his face?|The insistence asserts personal identity and dignity against an expert who behaves as though the face can be redesigned. Its earnestness in an absurd situation also produces humour.
Explain the irony in the photographer’s confidence about retouching.|He regards increasingly drastic alterations as achievements. For the narrator, each improvement makes the picture less useful as a likeness. Technical success becomes failure in the customer’s terms.
How does first-person narration contribute to the humour?|Readers follow the customer’s increasing discomfort and indignation directly. His restrained account of absurd professional behaviour exposes the photographer’s self-importance.
Why does the finished photograph fail even if its features are considered attractive?|Attractiveness is not the narrator’s main criterion. It fails to preserve his individuality and therefore cannot serve the personal and emotional purpose for which he wanted it.
How does the narrator’s reaction at the end expose the limits of professional expertise?|He rejects the altered portrait because it does not represent him. The expert’s ability to manipulate an image does not authorise him to replace the subject’s wishes.
Discuss the conflict between appearance and identity using the visit, the alterations and the ending.|The visit begins with a wish for a personal likeness. Repeated criticism and retouching replace the individual with a supposed ideal. The narrator’s rejection asserts that identity and recognisability matter more than imposed perfection.'''),
'The Elevator': ('Prose','Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.', '''Give three features of the elevator that contribute to Martin’s fear.|Its confined space, poor lighting and unreliable-seeming movement/closing mechanism make it threatening to him. Explain how each feature increases claustrophobia rather than merely listing adjectives.
How does Martin’s father respond to his fear?|He dismisses it as weakness and expects him to overcome it. The response makes Martin ashamed and leaves him unable to seek reassuring understanding.
Why is the woman’s physical presence particularly disturbing inside the elevator?|She occupies much of the small space and seems to stare at Martin. Her proximity combines his existing fear of confinement with uncertainty about her intentions.
How does using the stairs fail to solve Martin’s problem?|The stairs are also difficult and frightening to him; an accident injures him. Avoidance increases his vulnerability and eventually makes him more dependent on the elevator.
Why is Martin’s injury important to the development of suspense?|It limits his freedom of movement and makes escape or choosing the stairs harder. The physical injury turns emotional fear into greater practical helplessness.
Explain how repeated meetings with the woman affect the reader.|Each recurrence prevents the fear from disappearing and gives it an apparently inescapable pattern. Limited knowledge of the woman’s intentions sustains uncertainty.
What effect is produced by the woman’s final greeting to Martin?|Her knowledge of him and the private encounter intensify his fear. The ending stops without explaining her intentions or supplying a rescue, leaving the threat unresolved.
How do setting, parental response and the open ending work together?|The confined elevator creates fear; the father’s dismissal isolates Martin; injury restricts his choices. The unresolved final encounter leaves readers sharing uncertainty instead of receiving a complete explanation.'''),
'The Girl Who Can': ('Prose','Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.', '''What is Nana’s objection to Adjoa’s legs?|She judges them too thin and measures their value against traditional expectations of a woman’s body and future motherhood, rather than the child’s own abilities.
Why does Adjoa find adult conversation difficult to understand?|Adults use assumptions and disagreements they do not fully explain to her. Her child perspective reveals gaps between their confident claims and what she can observe.
How does Adjoa’s running challenge Nana’s judgement?|The legs dismissed as inadequate enable exceptional performance. Actual achievement overturns an appearance-based assessment and provides a different measure of capability.
Why is the school setting important to Adjoa’s development?|It creates an opportunity for her ability to be recognised through participation and performance. The opportunity extends beyond the domestic expectations surrounding her.
What does Nana’s response to Adjoa’s achievement reveal?|Pride and emotional involvement qualify her earlier criticism. She can respond to evidence and affection even while representing traditional assumptions.
How does the first-person child narrator expose prejudice without a long direct argument?|Readers compare Adjoa’s puzzled observations with the adults’ certainty. Her straightforward account allows the inconsistency of judging ability by appearance to emerge.
Explain the significance of the title in relation to Adjoa.|It affirms demonstrated ability rather than the limitations others assume. The child becomes someone who can achieve, despite judgements imposed on her body.
Compare Nana’s initial assessment with the outcome, and explain the story’s treatment of change.|Initially Nana assesses Adjoa through conventional physical expectations. Running supplies counter-evidence. Nana’s pride suggests attitudes can be challenged through experience, while Adjoa gains recognition on the basis of capability.'''),
'The Pedestrian': ('Prose','Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.', '''Why does the absence of other pedestrians matter?|It makes Mead’s ordinary walk appear exceptional. It also shows a society whose people withdraw into houses and screen entertainment, leaving public space empty.
How do descriptions of the houses contribute to the atmosphere?|The houses seem dark, silent and tomb-like apart from screen light. The imagery suggests isolation and diminished public life rather than a lively neighbourhood.
Why does the police car regard Mead’s profession as suspicious or incomprehensible?|His writing has little recognised place in a society dominated by screens. The machine treats deviation from the norm as a problem rather than valuing independent work.
How does the automated police car represent social authority?|Its impersonal questioning reduces Mead to a set of expected categories. Its lack of a human occupant emphasises mechanical control without sympathetic judgement.
Why is walking simply to observe considered abnormal in this society?|Leisure is expected to occur indoors with screens. Mead’s independent curiosity and unstructured movement do not fit the system’s prescribed behaviour.
What is ironic about the institution to which Mead is taken?|Ordinary independent behaviour is classified as a condition requiring correction. The society’s idea of normality is itself disturbing to the reader.
Why does the contrast between Mead’s home and the others matter near the end?|His brightly lit home contrasts with the surrounding darkness and symbolises a more active individual life. He is removed from that life by the conformity-enforcing system.
Explain how setting and the encounter develop the conflict between individuality and conformity.|Mead’s attentive walking contrasts with the empty screen-bound city. The police questions deny the legitimacy of harmless difference. His detention turns social sameness into coercion rather than a free preference.'''),
'The Last Lesson': ('Prose','Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.', '''Why is Franz initially reluctant to go to school?|He is unprepared for the lesson on participles and fears reprimand. The pleasant outdoors is more attractive to him than the classroom duties he has neglected.
What unusual features of the classroom signal a change?|The usual noise is absent, M. Hamel wears formal clothes and village elders sit at the back. These details prepare Franz for the seriousness of the final lesson.
What does Franz discover about the official order?|Only German is to be taught in the schools of Alsace and Lorraine. The lesson is therefore the last French lesson under M. Hamel.
How does Franz’s attitude towards his books change?|Formerly burdensome books become valued companions when he realises he may lose access to their language. Imminent loss makes him recognise neglected educational opportunities.
Why have the elders come to the classroom?|They regret neglecting education and wish to honour the teacher’s long service. Their attendance expresses respect for their language and shared identity.
How does M. Hamel distribute responsibility for neglected learning?|He criticises pupils’ postponement, parents’ preference for work and his own past lapses. The blame is shared rather than placed only on Franz.
Why is M. Hamel unable to complete his final spoken farewell?|Emotion overwhelms him. He turns to the blackboard and writes a patriotic declaration, making the silent action carry what he cannot say aloud.
Trace Franz’s change and connect it to language and identity.|Franz begins afraid of a school exercise and willing to avoid it. The order makes him understand language as a threatened shared inheritance. Attentive listening and regret replace indifference; the teacher’s final act gives that recognition public emotional force.'''),
'Haunted Houses': ('Poetry','The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.', '''How do the poem’s ghosts differ from frightening conventional ghosts?|They are quiet presences associated with former inhabitants rather than violent apparitions. The tone invites reflection on continuity and memory rather than terror.
Why are domestic spaces important to the poem?|Doorways, stairs and rooms connect everyday life with people who occupied them before. Familiar surroundings make the past seem close to the living.
How does the speaker question exclusive ownership of a house?|The living possess legal title, but the lives and memories of earlier occupants also belong to the place. Ownership is set against historical and emotional continuity.
What effect does the image of invisible guests produce?|It suggests that the present contains more lives and memories than the eye perceives. Ordinary social activity becomes a meeting of visible and invisible company.
How does the poem connect the material and spiritual worlds?|It presents them as overlapping rather than completely separate. Images of passage and connection allow the unseen to influence ordinary experience.
Why does the calm tone matter to the treatment of death?|It makes coexistence with the past contemplative and natural. The poem offers continuity rather than a sensational supernatural threat.
What does the title suggest once the whole poem is considered?|Being haunted means retaining traces of earlier lives. The title broadens from a ghost-story expectation into a meditation on memory and spiritual presence.
Explain how two images develop the continuing relationship between past and present.|Discuss quiet movement through familiar rooms and the presence of unseen guests, or another accurate image. Explain how each joins former inhabitants to present life, and relate the non-threatening tone to the poem’s understanding of continuity.'''),
'The Glove and the Lions': ('Poetry','At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.', '''Why is the setting at a royal court important?|The spectators and king make the act a public display. The lady’s demand is shaped by the possibility of gaining attention and admiration before an audience.
How are the lions presented before the glove is dropped?|Their violent movement and power establish genuine physical danger. This makes the test a serious misuse of courage rather than a harmless game.
What does the lady expect her lover to prove?|She expects him to retrieve the glove as evidence of devotion and to make her the admired centre of a spectacular romantic gesture.
Why does the lover retrieve the glove but reject the lady?|Retrieval demonstrates courage; rejection refuses a relationship in which vanity endangers him. He distinguishes bravery from submission to an unreasonable test.
What does the king’s response contribute to the ending?|It endorses the rejection and exposes the lady’s vanity. Public judgement reverses the admiration she hoped the stunt would win.
Explain the irony of the lady’s plan.|She seeks a public proof of love that will enhance her prestige. The successful retrieval instead becomes the occasion for public rejection.
How does the final action change the meaning of the heroic rescue?|The danger is overcome, but the emotional reward is refused. The act affirms self-respect rather than the lady’s power to demand sacrifice.
Discuss the distinction between love and vanity in the lady’s test and its outcome.|The test values spectacle over the beloved’s safety. The lover shows courage but refuses that condition of affection. The king’s approval makes the closing judgement favour dignity and sincere care over theatrical display.'''),
'When Great Trees Fall': ('Poetry','The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.', '''What does the image of a great tree represent in the poem?|It represents a person whose life gives others shelter, direction or strength. The scale of the tree suggests the breadth of the loss caused by such a death.
Why does the opening describe reactions in the natural world?|The disturbed animals and landscape give physical scale to loss. The analogy prepares readers for the emotional and intellectual disruption caused by a great soul’s death.
How does the poem represent the immediate effect of bereavement on perception?|The world seems altered and difficult to grasp; familiar sources of security and understanding weaken. Accurate reference to the poem’s images should support the response.
What role does memory play after the death of a great person?|Memory recalls the person’s presence and the opportunities for appreciation that were missed. It makes absence painful while preserving the influence of the life.
Why does the poem’s movement towards renewal not cancel grief?|Grief remains the starting reality. Renewal grows from recognising the continuing possibilities inspired by the person, rather than pretending that the loss did not occur.
How does the shift in tone develop the poem’s argument?|The early disturbance and diminished perception give way to a more affirmative recognition of existence and responsibility. The change enacts gradual recovery.
What responsibility do the living acquire through remembering great souls?|They can live more fully and recognise capacities awakened by those lives. Continuing influence becomes a reason to act rather than remain permanently diminished.
Trace the movement from the tree image to the closing affirmation.|A large physical loss models the disruption of an influential death. Human grief then affects perception and memory. The later recovery recognises that the person’s existence enlarges the possibilities of those who remain, allowing affirmation without erasing sorrow.'''),
'A Considerable Speck': ('Poetry','A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.', '''Why is the speck first easy to dismiss?|Its tiny size makes it appear insignificant and it is initially seen against the writer’s working page. Close observation challenges that first judgement.
Which aspect of the speck’s behaviour suggests intelligence to the speaker?|Its apparently purposeful movement, hesitation and response to danger seem different from mechanical motion. The inference comes from observed behaviour rather than size.
How does the writer’s control over the page create a moral choice?|He could destroy the creature easily while writing. Recognising signs of an independent mind makes restraint an ethical decision rather than a physical necessity.
Why is the title an apparent contradiction?|A speck is minute, whereas considerable suggests importance. The poem separates physical smallness from the significance of thought or life.
How does humour affect the speaker’s reflection?|The disproportion between a writer and a tiny visitor makes the meditation playful. It avoids pompous moralising while still taking perceived intelligence seriously.
Why is the poem more than an observation of an insect?|The encounter prompts reflection on judgement, intelligence and the treatment of a weaker being. The creature’s size tests the speaker’s willingness to recognise value.
What does the decision to spare the speck reveal?|It reveals respect for a perceived independent mind and the deliberate restraint of power. The speaker values mental life without requiring physical grandeur.
Explain how observation changes the speaker’s response and supports the title.|What initially seems negligible displays purposeful behaviour. The speaker revises his judgement and chooses to spare it. The creature becomes considerable through the intelligence he perceives, giving the title its comic and moral force.'''),
'The Power of Music': ('Poetry','Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.', '''What makes the singer’s confidence comic?|He is absorbed in his performance while the surrounding world reacts with distress. The mismatch between self-satisfaction and actual effect undermines his view of his talent.
How do exaggerated geographical effects create humour?|The voice appears to disturb an impossibly wide area. Hyperbole magnifies an unpleasant performance into a fantastic public calamity.
Why are the reactions of animals and people presented together?|They make the suffering seem universal and extend the joke beyond an individual listener’s taste. The whole environment becomes a witness against the singer.
How does the poem treat appeals to stop the singing?|Ordinary responses fail to restrain the performer. Repetition of distress and his persistence prepare for an unexpected physical interruption.
Why is the goat’s intervention an effective ending?|An animal accomplishes what the listeners cannot. Its direct physical action abruptly ends the inflated musical ordeal and reverses the performer’s dominance.
What is ironic about the title?|Music conventionally suggests pleasure or harmony. Here its power is represented as disruptive and painful, making the title praise the performance in a comic opposite sense.
Explain the role of sound and rhythm in the comic narrative.|Energetic movement and sound effects reproduce the commotion caused by the song. Discuss accurate examples from the prescribed translation and link form to escalating disturbance.
How do hyperbole, contrast and the final reversal work together?|Impossible effects inflate the song into a catastrophe; the singer’s delight contrasts with widespread distress. A goat’s simple action defeats the supposedly powerful performer, completing the satire of self-important bad singing.''')
}

def make_bank():
    bank=[]
    for title,(section,context,rows) in TEXTS.items():
        for i,row in enumerate(rows.splitlines()):
            prompt,answer=row.split('|',1)
            bank.append(item(title,prompt,answer,4 if i==7 else 3,id=f'english2-{len(bank)+1:03}',section=section,family=title,context=context))
    return bank
